  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1136 PM CDT MON SEP 21 2026\n\nA marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe storms remains in\neffect across Central Alabama through this upcoming evening as a\npronounced upper-level shortwave trough moves northeastward\ntoward the Great Lakes, driving an accompanying surface cold\nfront into the region from the northwest. The primary severe\nweather concern will be localized damaging downburst winds \ncapable of knocking down tree limbs, along with periods of \ntorrential rainfall that could cause temporary low- lying street \nflooding in heavier cells.\n\nAs the cold front slowly slides through the area into Wednesday,\nshower and thunderstorm activity will gradually taper off from\nnorthwest to southeast, clearing the way for a notable pattern\nshift just in time for the autumnal equinox. A cooler, drier\ncontinental airmass will settle into the Southeast behind the\nfront, keeping daytime highs in the lower-to-middle 80s on\nThursday and Friday. Overnight temperatures will drop into the\nupper 50s across northern portions of the area, with lower-to-\nmiddle 60s persisting further south. This dry, highly seasonable,\nand early fall regime will persist right through the upcoming\nweekend, offering calm conditions and clear to partly cloudy\nskies across all of Central Alabama.\n\n16",
